+    # reference_price is written exactly twice: when a branch is created, and
+    # thereafter only on a real fill. Nothing moves it toward the market. So a
+    # flat branch that has not traded through a rally measures its next buy
+    # from a level the market already left - and since no fill means no
+    # re-anchor, a rising market makes the next fill HARDER, not easier.
+    #
+    # Live on 2026-09-25 at a 2.50% step, six flat branches were waiting for
+    # falls of 2.89% to 4.80%. ARB needed 4.80%, nearly twice its own spacing.
+    #
+    # reanchor_flat_grid_branches_now() already does this correctly and is
+    # wired to a dashboard button. A button is a one-shot: references go stale
+    # again the moment the market moves, so the fleet needs it every cycle
+    # rather than whenever somebody notices.
+    #
+    # Same safety rules as that function, enforced here rather than inherited:
+    # FLAT branches only (with a slice open the reference is ALSO the sell
+    # trigger, so moving it would move the exit of a live position), and
+    # UPWARD only (a lower reference is a lower buy trigger, i.e. further from
+    # the market, so a branch already below its reference is nearer a fill
